    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d641">

                <pb facs="0615" n="531" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp02/pg531"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.48/589</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Sweden</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Morris</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stockholm</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-05-15T17:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 15, 1918, 5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received May 16, 2.50 a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2092. Your circular telegram 767, May 13.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d641-f1"/> I am
                    inclined to think that any relief money sent into portions of Russia now
                    occupied by the enemy will not bring any beneficial results to the Allied cause,
                    but on the contrary the non-sending of same would tend to increase the already
                    existing ill-feeling against Germany, the population then basing non-receipt of
                    relief as due to the German occupation.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Morris</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
